WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.830
A continental hotspot connects processes above and below the surface.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.090
In the textbook model, hot, mostly solid mantle rises toward the

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:14.270
lithosphere. Decompression produces partial melting, while heat can also cause melting

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:19.130
in surrounding material. Heating and thinning the lithosphere encourage broad uplift.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:22.390
The rising mantle is not itself a fully molten column.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.640
Doming and stretching can organize into three rift arms.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.740
Normal faults lower blocks into basins, which collect sediment and volcanic

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.340
rocks. Basaltic magma may erupt as extensive flows;

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.500
heating continental crust can generate more felsic magma.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.460
In Afar, the Red Sea, Gulf of Aden, and East African

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.580
Rift meet. The two seas have spreading centers, but the text

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.560
does not guarantee that the African rift will split the continent

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:47.160
completely.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:52.110
Yellowstone marks the interpreted current hotspot location in the supplied model.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:56.470
The Snake River Plain records North America's southwestward movement over it.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.390
Large caldera-forming eruptions shaped Yellowstone's history, while retained heat drives its

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.570
geothermal features. The nearby Basin and Range contains extension and fault

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:08.790
blocks. An age-progressive track records relative movement;

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.850
do not reverse plate motion and hotspot-track direction.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.280
In one common evolutionary sequence, two rift arms open into a

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.920
new ocean while the third fails to separate the continent.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.260
Rivers can follow the low failed rift and deliver sediment to

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.560
a delta. Continued spreading moves the ridge away from the continental

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.600
edge. The edge cools and subsides, becoming a passive margin because

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.700
the active plate boundary is now offshore.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:36.850
The inward bend along western equatorial Africa illustrates how rifting can

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.110
influence a continental outline. Two arms helped open the South Atlantic;

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.330
the failed arm remained within Africa.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.610
Use the map and the cross sections together:

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:50.230
the map shows the branching geometry, while the sections explain thinning,

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:53.070
new ocean formation, and later subsidence.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:57.250
This is an evolutionary model, not a promise that every hotspot

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.650
follows every step.
